<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Zubayr's Bahr al-Ghazal</h1><p class="st-meta">1856 CE – 1874 CE (approx.) · capital Dem Zubayr</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The private trading and slaving empire of the merchant Zubayr Rahma Mansur in south-western Sudan, which grew strong enough to conquer Darfur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zubayr-rahma-mansur">Zubayr Rahma Mansur</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaalin">Ja'ali</a> merchant from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-al-ghazal">Bahr al-Ghazal</a> region around 1856 to trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a>. He built a chain of fortified stations, called zaribas, and made Dem Zubayr his headquarters, forming alliances with local <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zande-kingdoms">Azande</a> and other chiefs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zubayr's power rested on a private army of enslaved soldiers armed with firearms, known as bazingers. His network traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and enslaved people north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He defeated an Egyptian force sent against him, and in 1873 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a> recognised him as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-al-ghazal">Bahr al-Ghazal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1874 Zubayr invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ibrahim and handing the country to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Detained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> from 1875, he lost control of his domain; his son Sulayman rebelled and was killed in 1879.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1856 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.64%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1856 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dem Zubayr</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zubayr Rahma Mansur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zubayr Pasha's state, Dem Zubayr</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=zubayrs-bahr-al-ghazal" aria-label="Zubayr's Bahr al-Ghazal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5521 1907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zubayr's Bahr al-Ghazal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5841 2163h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5841 2163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Sudan. <a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=zubayrs-bahr-al-ghazal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
